  250  * Data Movement APIs.
  251  *
  252  * See block comment above for userland data movement APIs for general
  253  * concepts.  The main differences here are the kern_channel_notify()
  254  * and kern_channel_reclaim() calls that aren't available for userland.
  255  * These calls are typically invoked within the TX and RX sync callbacks
  256  * implemented by the nexus provider.
  257  *
  258  * For TX sync, kern_channel_reclaim() is normally called after the
  259  * provider has finished reclaiming slots that have been "transmitted".
  260  * In this case, this call is simply a way to indicate to the system
  261  * that such condition has happened.
  262  *
  263  * For RX sync, kern_channel_reclaim() must be called at the beginning
  264  * of the callback in order to reclaim user-released slots, and to
  265  * ensure that subsequent calls to kern_channel_available_slot_count()
  266  * or kern_channel_get_next_slot() operates on the most recent state.
  267  *
  268  * The kern_channel_notify() is used to post notifications to indicate
  269  * slot availability; this may result in the kernel event subsystem
  270  * posting readable and writable events.
  271  */
